{
  "//": [
    "The application, in TypeScript. Nothing here runs directly: the browser",
    "cannot execute TypeScript, so `npm run build:web` compiles it into js/ and",
    "index.html loads that. The compiled js/ is generated, not edited, and is",
    "committed so the repository can be opened without installing anything -",
    "which is also why the page has no import map and no module graph: the",
    "output is one classic script per source file, in the order index.html asks",
    "for it, exactly as before the port."
  ],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "ES2022",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "lib": ["ES2022", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "strict": true,
    // Off on purpose, and the only strict flag that is. It asks for a type on
    // every parameter of a 3,000-line codebase, and annotating those
    // mechanically buys nothing a reader has to read. Everything that catches a
    // real defect is on: null-safety, property existence, argument counts,
    // dead code, and fallthrough. Turning this one on later is a mechanical
    // afternoon.
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": false,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": false,
    "noEmit": true,
    "useDefineForClassFields": false
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist", "js", "src-tauri", "artifacts", "shells"]
}